.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt SERVICE 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm service
.Nd run an init script the way boot runs it
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar name
.Ar action
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl -status-all
.Nm
.Fl -list
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs
.Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/ Ns Ar name
with
.Ar action ,
and the point of the command is the environment.
A service started by hand gets the one it would get at boot rather than
whatever the shell it was typed into happens to be carrying: an inherited
.Ev LD_PRELOAD ,
a
.Ev PATH
with
.Pa /usr/local/bin
at the front, or a variable set for an unrelated reason are all things a daemon
would then keep for as long as it runs, and the machine would behave one way
after a manual restart and another way after a reboot.
.Pp
The environment a script is given is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
PATH=/bin:/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/sbin
HOME=/
PWD=/
TERM=$TERM   (or "linux")
LANG=$LANG   (or "C")
.Ed
.Pp
.Ev TERM
and
.Ev LANG
are carried across so the output is readable.
Everything else is dropped.
.Pp
The working directory is part of this, and
.Nm
changes to
.Pa /
before running the script, because that is what a service inherits at boot:
.Xr init 8
never changes directory, so
.Pa /
is the working directory of process 1 and of everything it starts.
A service left in the directory it was started from is one whose daemon holds
that directory open for as long as it runs, which is a filesystem that cannot
then be unmounted.
.Pp
Any
.Ar argument
after the action is passed to the script.
Most take none; the two VPN services take the name of one tunnel or interface,
so that
.Ic service openvpn restart office
acts on that tunnel alone and leaves the others running.
.Ss Actions
The action is whatever the init script implements, and
.Nm
neither checks it nor knows what it means.
Every Sowa init script has these:
.Bl -tag -width restart -offset indent
.It Cm start
start the service, unless it is already running.
.It Cm stop
stop it.
.It Cm restart
stop it and start it again.
.It Cm status
say whether it is running, and exit 0 if it is.
.El
.Pp
Some have more:
.Cm reload
where the daemon can be told to reread its configuration without being
restarted.
A script run with no action, or with one it does not implement, prints what it
accepts and exits 2.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--status-all"
.It Fl -list
Print the name of every init script in
.Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d ,
whether or not it starts at boot.
.It Fl -status-all
Run
.Cm status
on each of them, in that order.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/rc.d/init.d" -compact
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d
the init scripts.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/init.d/functions
the shell library each one sources.
.It Pa /run/lock/subsys
one file per running service.
This is how the runlevel driver knows a service is already up, so entering a
runlevel twice does not start anything twice.
.It Pa /var/log/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .log
where a service's output goes.
Services are kept in the foreground under
.Xr setsid 1
and their standard output and standard error are appended to a file of their
own.
This is separate from what
.Xr syslog-ng 8
collects: that is what programs send to
.Pa /dev/log ,
while this is what a service says on its own descriptors, which is where the
reason it would not start is.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The exit status is the init script's.
.Cm status
exits 0 when the service is running.
.Nm
itself exits 1 when there is no such service and 2 when its own arguments make
no sense.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
service --status-all
service sshd restart
service openvpn start office
service network status
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr inittab 5 ,
.Xr chkconfig 8 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr runlevel 8
.Sh CAVEATS
.Nm
does nothing about boot: a service started here is running now and will not be
running after a reboot unless
.Xr chkconfig 8
has been told to start it.
The two commands are deliberately separate.
